"Examines the structural forces that shape competition and the distribution of economic value within an industry.\n\nUse when: Assess the attractiveness of a defined industry. Explain how entry, substitutes and bargaining power affect strategic position.\n\nAvoid when: The primary question is internal efficiency or individual competitor features.\n\nVisual grammar: Industry rivalry occupies the focal region. Four surrounding forces represent entrants, substitutes, suppliers and buyers. Short evidence notes state the structural drivers of each force.\n\nBuild: Define the industry boundary and relevant buyer and supplier groups. Identify the economic drivers behind each force. Discuss likely changes and positioning options supported by those drivers.\n\nCheck: Substitutes meet the same need through a different approach. Industry structure is distinguished from one firm's capabilities. Claims about bargaining power include evidence and scope.\n\nPitfalls: Listing brand names without analyzing structural pressure. Calling every adjacent firm a direct rival or substitute.\n\nExample: A regional cinema examines streaming substitutes, distributor bargaining power, local entry barriers and audience switching options before choosing its offer.\n\nAlternatives: Value chain, PESTLE analysis."
